# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRODUCTION CONFIG — TEMPLATE ONLY. NO REAL SECRETS IN THIS FILE, EVER.
#
# This file is COMMITTED. It documents WHICH variables a production deploy must
# supply, not what their values are. On Heroku the real values live in config
# vars (`heroku config:set NAME=value`), which arrive in the process
# environment — and `src/env.ts` never overwrites a variable that is already
# set, so config vars always win over any file that happens to be present.
#
# ONE codebase, switched by config:
#   local dev/demo → codecep-server/.env.local   (gitignored, real values)
#   production     → Heroku config vars          (no env file deployed)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Postgres. On Heroku this is provisioned by the Heroku Postgres add-on and set
# automatically; Heroku's URL requires SSL, unlike the local instance.
DATABASE_URL=<<heroku postgres url — set by the Heroku Postgres add-on>>

# Redis for the BullMQ forensics queue. The worker needs a REAL Redis — it is
# never stubbed (see CLAUDE.md Constraint 6).
REDIS_URL=<<heroku redis url — set by the Heroku Data for Redis add-on>>

# JWT signing secret. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Rotating this invalidates every issued token (7d expiry).
JWT_SECRET=<<set in Heroku config vars — never commit a real secret>>

# Gemini (syllabus → per-week allowlist). Required at startup: the server
# throws if it is missing.
GEMINI_API_KEY=<<set in Heroku config vars — never commit a real key>>

# NOT NEEDED for the SINGLE-APP deploy (the default): the server serves the
# built client, so the browser's calls are SAME-ORIGIN, and same-origin is
# always allowed — including the POSTs that do carry an Origin header. Leave it
# unset unless the client is hosted somewhere else.
#
# Which browser origins may call this server — the deployed CLIENT's origin.
# Comma-separated for more than one (e.g. a custom domain plus the herokuapp
# one), matched EXACTLY on the full origin. Enforced for both the HTTP API and
# the Socket.io handshake, so getting this wrong takes out the live DVR and the
# Tier-1 alert stream as well as ordinary requests.
#
# Setting it REPLACES the localhost default — production is not reachable from a
# developer's local page. If the Express server also serves the built client
# (the single-app deploy recommended in CLAUDE.md §7.10), requests are
# same-origin and carry no Origin header, which is allowed regardless; set this
# anyway so a separately-hosted client keeps working.
CORS_ORIGIN=<<https://your-app.herokuapp.com>>

# ── CLIENT build-time (NOT a Heroku config var) ─────────────────────────────
# The client reads VITE_API_URL, which Vite INLINES at BUILD time — setting it
# as a runtime config var does nothing. It must be present in the environment
# that runs `npm run build` in codecep-client/. See codecep-client/.env.example.
#   VITE_API_URL=https://your-app.herokuapp.com

# Heroku assigns PORT at boot and it must be read from the environment, not
# hardcoded. Listed here for completeness; do NOT set it in config vars.
# PORT=<<assigned by Heroku>>

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Judge0 execution endpoint. NOT read from the environment yet — the endpoint is
# still hardcoded to https://ce.judge0.com in src/server.ts. Setting this today
# would do nothing. Self-hosting Judge0 (go-local Part 2) is what introduces it.
# JUDGE0_URL=<<prod judge0 endpoint>>

# Set to true ONLY when a real reverse proxy sits in front of the app (Heroku's
# router does). Without a proxy in front, trusting X-Forwarded-For lets any
# client claim its own address, which defeats the network restriction
# (CLAUDE.md gap #45).
TRUST_PROXY=true

# Per-request debug traces. Leave OFF in production.
# DEBUG=false
